{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>And the חומט and the תנשמת.</b> It mentions eight creeping things and says of them, 'these are they which are unclean to you,' and the serpent is not included among them. And one may wonder at this, for it would certainly have been fitting for the serpent to be unclean and defiling, since it is the root of impurity and pollution, as is known from the primordial serpent. If so, why did Scripture not include it among the unclean creeping things?" "<b>But</b> the matter is among the general principles of the Torah's ways, all whose paths are peace: it did not wish to render the serpent unclean by its touch, for if so a person would refrain from killing it in order not to become defiled. And for this reason it mentioned 'whatsoever goes upon the belly' — which is the serpent — and 'whatsoever goes upon four' — which is the scorpion — and 'multiplies feet' — which is the creature whose name is נדל — because they are creatures harmful to human beings through their venom, some making ill and some killing, and it said of them, 'you shall not eat them, for they are an abomination': it forbade only eating but declared their touch pure, unlike the eight creeping things that have no venom at all."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:24:49.598470793Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}